## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
## campaign (Campy) — supervise a WHOLE modernisation programme,
## lot after lot, holding the separation of powers a human held before.
##
## `modernize` carries ONE lot per run, by design: the programme is a suite
## of runs, not one long run. Someone has to relaunch it, judge whether a run
## landed anything, execute the ledger requests the worker is forbidden to
## execute, and decide when the programme is over. This bot is that someone.
##
## It was measured before it was written. A full modernisation programme was
## replayed end to end under a human supervisor, and the interventions were
## counted: almost all of them were MECHANICAL — a written procedure with a
## verifiable criterion — and the criterion held on every single act: the
## observed reference diff equalled the announced one, every time. What was
## mechanical is what this bot mechanises. What was not stays with a human,
## through two escalation modes.
##
## THE SUPERVISOR IS DETERMINISTIC. There is not one LLM node in this graph:
## intelligence lives in the child bots, judgement lives in gates, and the
## supervisor only measures, acts on written announcements, and keeps a
## journal. A supervisor with opinions would be a third author in a system
## whose whole safety comes from having exactly two, separated.
##
## THE TWO AUTHORITIES, unchanged:
##   - the worker (`modernize`, run as a subbot) changes the code and NEVER
##     writes the oracle's references — its own gate enforces that in git;
##   - the steward (a tool node HERE) re-records references, and ONLY on a
##     request written to the ledger, ONLY when the observed diff equals the
##     announced set exactly, with the full counter-test replayed behind.
##   The window discipline that separation demands — never act while a
##   worker is writing — is structural here: a subbot node returns only when
##   the child run is over. What cost a human a suspend/wait/verify ritual
##   costs this graph nothing.
##
## PROGRESS IS JUDGED BY GIT, NEVER BY THE RUN'S OWN JOURNAL. A run that did
## not move HEAD landed nothing — whatever it says of itself. Two child runs
## in a row without movement end the campaign: the point where a programme
## stops moving IS the measurement.
## ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

dsl: 2

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"
  ## The programme contract, versioned in the TARGET repo.
  plan_path: string = ".modernize/plan.yaml"
  ## Who approves a CONTRACT EXTENSION — a lot the worker added or reshaped
  ## in the plan, beyond flipping its own status. "bot": accepted in flight,
  ## listed at the HEAD of the end-of-campaign handoff for review. "human":
  ## every extension pauses the campaign for an operator, whatever the
  ## escalation mode — an unapproved extension shapes every lot after it,
  ## so its approval cannot wait for the end.
  governance: string [enum: "bot", "human"] = "bot"
  ## When escalation items other than extensions arise (a rebaseline request
  ## the steward refused, a mismatched diff, a red counter-test, a dirty
  ## tree): "interactive" pauses the run there and then; "handoff"
  ## accumulates them — a blocked lot no longer blocks its dependants, so
  ## the campaign keeps landing what it still can — and presents everything
  ## once, at the end.
  escalation: string [enum: "handoff", "interactive"] = "handoff"
  ## Upper bound on the campaign loop's crossings: at most max_lots + 1 child
  ## runs, 0 for one. The natural stops are the programme being exhausted or
  ## the stagnation rule; this is the belt over those braces.
  max_lots: int = 40
  ## Consecutive child runs without a single new commit that end the
  ## campaign. Two is the measured value: one still run can be a lot that
  ## legitimately blocked; two in a row means nothing eligible is landing.
  stagnation_stop: int = 2
  ## Forwarded to the child: bounded repair loop per lot.
  lot_max_passes: int = 4
  ## The campaign time one lot may take, in seconds: lot_room starts a lot
  ## only while the elapsed time plus this room stays under 90% of the
  ## campaign's wall, from which the engine refuses to start a tool node, the
  ## handoff's (finalize) included; the child judges the same where it starts
  ## (modernize's campaign_room). 54000 s = 15h: modernize's 12h wall and the
  ## exit grace past it (13.2h), one golden-master child its last gate routes
  ## to (1h wall, 1.1h), then 0.7h for the campaign to reach its handoff:
  ## observe and the steward outside its acts, 1560 s at their commands'
  ## walls. A steward act starts only where it, its git work and the journal
  ## commit end before that point (RESERVE_S), and finalize cuts what the
  ## campaign's wall would and writes the handoff either way. The exit grace
  ## is the deployment's default 10% (ITERION_BUDGET_EXIT_GRACE): a wider one
  ## lengthens the longest lot — 12h and 1h, each times one plus the grace —
  ## and this must grow with it. A wall with no room for one lot starts none,
  ## and the handoff says so with these figures: a validation run under a
  ## short --max-duration sizes this to its lot plus those 1560 s (README,
  ## "Configuration"). Lower it for a contract whose lots are known to be
  ## short.
  lot_room_s: int = 54000

secrets:
  ## The forge credential of a repo-targeted cloud launch. The runner clones
  ## the target repository for THIS run, before preflight, with the token the
  ## launch resolves — and a launch resolves only the secrets the launched
  ## bot declares: its forge_token override fills a declared name or
  ## nothing. Undeclared, a private repository's clone runs credential-less
  ## and fails on "could not read Username" before any node starts. run_lot's
  ## modernize child runs on the credentials resolved HERE, so its own
  ## declaration of the same secret reaches it through this one. optional:
  ## local runs keep host-authenticated git.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

## ─── Prompts (for the two HUMAN nodes — no LLM reads these) ─────────
prompt escalate_instructions:
  The campaign supervisor paused because the items under review need an
  operator. They are listed with their type and evidence.

  Anything that must change, change IN THE REPOSITORY — edit the plan,
  answer a ledger request by hand, clean the tree — and commit it. The
  supervisor re-reads reality from git on the next run; nothing you type
  here edits anything.

  Then answer: `continue` to resume the campaign, or `stop` to end it now
  and go straight to the final handoff.

prompt review_instructions:
  The campaign is over and its handoff is committed in the repository. It
  leads with every contract extension that was accepted in flight under the
  configured governance — review those first: they are decisions a bot took
  and the handoff exists so a human re-takes them. Below them: escalations
  and their answers, the steward's acts, the run journal, the final oracle
  verdict, and the requalification of blocked lots.

  Acknowledge to close the run.

## ─── Schemas ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
schema preflight_state:
  campaign_base: string
  refs_dir: string
  verify_path: string
  ledger_path: string
  notice: string

schema observe_input:
  base: string
  ledger_path: string

schema observe_state:
  before: string
  moved: bool
  stagnant: int
  exhausted: bool
  extensions: json
  pending: json
  notice: string

schema steward_input:
  before: string
  moved: bool
  extensions: json
  pending: json
  ledger_path: string
  verify_path: string

## `pause_required` is computed from configuration and facts, in code the
## operator can read — not by a model, and not by the child being judged.
schema steward_state:
  acts: int
  escalations: json
  pause_required: bool
  notice: string

schema escalate_input:
  items: string
  context: string

schema escalate_answer:
  action: string [enum: "continue", "stop"]
  note: string

schema answer_state:
  stop: bool

schema loop_state:
  proceed: bool
  reason: string

schema room_state:
  room: bool
  reason: string

## `ended_by` is why the campaign ended, as the edge that reached finalize
## knows it: the handoff and its notice lead with it.
schema finalize_input:
  verify_path: string
  ended_by: string

schema finalize_state:
  handoff_path: string
  oracle_green: bool
  lots_done: int
  lots_blocked: int
  requalified: int
  notice: string

schema review_input:
  handoff_path: string
  summary: string

schema review_ack:
  ack: string

## escalate — the in-flight window, when configuration asks for one.
##
## The answer edits nothing: an operator who wants to change the programme
## changes the repository and commits, exactly as a human supervisor did.
## The pause IS the window; git carries the intervention.
human escalate:
  description: "Campaign escalation — operator review during the run"
  input: escalate_input
  output: escalate_answer
  interaction: human
  instructions: escalate_instructions

## handoff_review — both escalation modes end here: the campaign never
## closes without offering a human the decisions it took.
human handoff_review:
  description: "End-of-campaign handoff review"
  input: review_input
  output: review_ack
  interaction: human
  instructions: review_instructions

## ─── Nodes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## preflight — refuse early what would make every later verdict a lie.
##
## A campaign over no contract is nothing to supervise; a campaign with no
## behavioural net would report lots "done" against nothing; a dirty tree at
## start means somebody's work in flight, which a supervisor must never
## sweep into its own commits or judge as the child's progress. All three
## REFUSE rather than emit a clean no-op: a supervisor that finishes green
## having supervised nothing is the blind judge this whole family of bots
## exists to refuse.
tool preflight:
  script: |
    import json
    import os
    import subprocess
    import sys

    ws = {{vars.workspace_dir}}
    plan_path = os.path.join(ws, {{vars.plan_path}})

    out = {"campaign_base": "", "refs_dir": "", "verify_path": "",
           "ledger_path": "", "notice": ""}

    def refuse(notice):
        out["notice"] = notice
        print(json.dumps(out))
        sys.stderr.write("campaign: %s\n" % notice)
        raise SystemExit(1)

    # Same lookup as the worker: PATH ONLY. yq is declared in this bundle's
    # devbox.json — being declared says which version should serve, not that
    # the environment was realised for this node. There is no fallback into
    # the workspace: <ws>/.devbox is the target repository's own devbox
    # profile, so a binary found there is an executable the target repo
    # supplied, run with this run's environment (#1799) — the same defect
    # this bundle refuses for its skills.
    def find_yq():
        import shutil
        return shutil.which("yq") or ""

    if not os.path.isfile(plan_path):
        refuse("no programme contract at %s. A campaign supervises a written "
               "programme; write the plan first (see the modernize bot's "
               "plan-contract skill)." % plan_path)

    yq = find_yq()
    if not yq:
        refuse("yq is not on PATH, so the contract at %s cannot be read. It "
               "is declared in this bundle's devbox.json — being declared "
               "says which version should serve, not that the environment "
               "was realised for this node." % plan_path)
    raw = subprocess.run([yq, "-o=json", plan_path], capture_output=True,
                         text=True, timeout=60)
    if raw.returncode != 0:
        refuse("the contract at %s is not parseable: %s" % (plan_path, raw.stderr[-800:]))
    plan = json.loads(raw.stdout)

    oracle = plan.get("oracle") or {}
    out["refs_dir"] = oracle.get("refs_dir") or ""
    oracle_dir = oracle.get("dir") or ".golden-master"
    out["verify_path"] = oracle.get("verify") or os.path.join(oracle_dir, "verify-oracle.sh")
    out["ledger_path"] = os.path.join(oracle_dir, "REBASELINE.md")

    if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(ws, out["verify_path"])):
        refuse("no behavioural oracle at %s. Supervising a programme without "
               "a net would report lots done against nothing; build the net "
               "first (golden-master bot)." % out["verify_path"])

    def is_scaffold(line):
        # iterion lays the bundle's skills (and plugin commands, agents,
        # settings) under <workspace>/.claude/ at run start: written by the
        # engine, never by a pass, never the run's work (#1364) -- the rule
        # finalize applies to the wip bank (runOutputPaths), tracked or not:
        # the engine rewrites a tracked settings.json to inject plugin hooks,
        # and a reader that refused on that write would stop every run on a
        # repository that commits it. A rename is judged on its destination;
        # a quoted path is unquoted.
        path = line[3:]
        if ' -> ' in path:
            path = path.split(' -> ', 1)[1]
        path = path.strip()
        if len(path) >= 2 and path[0] == chr(34) and path[-1] == chr(34):
            path = path[1:-1]
        return path.startswith('.claude/')
    st = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "status", "--porcelain"],
                        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    if st.returncode != 0:
        refuse("cannot read git status in %s: %s" % (ws, st.stderr[-400:]))
    # The engine materialises the executing node's own script into the
    # workspace (.iterion-script-*.py) for the lifetime of the node, so a
    # script tool ALWAYS sees that one untracked path. Execution machinery,
    # not work in flight — the one exclusion every status read here carries.
    dirty = [l for l in st.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()
             and not os.path.basename(l[3:].strip().strip('"')).startswith(".iterion-script-")
             and not is_scaffold(l)]
    if dirty:
        refuse("the workspace has %d uncommitted change(s) at campaign start "
               "(e.g. %s). A supervisor only ever acts between runs on "
               "committed trees, and it must not adopt work in flight. Commit "
               "or clean first." % (len(dirty), "; ".join(dirty[:5])))

    rev = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "rev-parse", "HEAD"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    if rev.returncode != 0:
        refuse("cannot read HEAD in %s: %s" % (ws, rev.stderr[-400:]))
    out["campaign_base"] = rev.stdout.strip()

    print(json.dumps(out))

  language: py
  output: preflight_state

## observe — everything the supervisor knows, re-read from git and files.
##
## Cross-iteration state lives in the repository (journal, ledger, plan),
## never in loop-carried messages: a campaign interrupted anywhere can be
## resumed, or re-run, and re-derive exactly where it stood.
tool observe:
  script: |
    import json
    import os
    import subprocess
    import tempfile

    ws = {{vars.workspace_dir}}
    plan_rel = {{vars.plan_path}}
    campaign_dir = os.path.join(ws, ".modernize", "campaign")
    journal = os.path.join(campaign_dir, "journal.tsv")

    out = {"before": "", "moved": False, "stagnant": 0, "exhausted": False,
           "extensions": [], "pending": [], "notice": ""}

    def refuse(notice):
        import sys
        out["notice"] = notice
        print(json.dumps(out))
        sys.stderr.write("campaign: %s\n" % notice)
        raise SystemExit(1)

    # PATH ONLY (preflight's find_yq carries the reason): a fallback under
    # <ws>/.devbox would run a binary the target repository supplied (#1799).
    def find_yq():
        import shutil
        return shutil.which("yq") or ""

    def git(*args, timeout=60):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws] + list(args),
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)

    yq = find_yq()
    if not yq:
        refuse("yq disappeared between preflight and observe")

    def read_plan(text):
        with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("w", suffix=".yaml", delete=False) as f:
            f.write(text)
            tmp = f.name
        try:
            raw = subprocess.run([yq, "-o=json", tmp], capture_output=True,
                                 text=True, timeout=60)
            if raw.returncode != 0:
                return None
            return json.loads(raw.stdout)
        finally:
            os.unlink(tmp)

    # The segment to judge: from the last supervised state to HEAD now.
    rows = []
    if os.path.isfile(journal):
        with open(journal, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            lines = [l.rstrip("\n") for l in f if l.strip()]
        rows = [l.split("\t") for l in lines[1:]]
    before = rows[-1][5] if rows else {{input.base}}
    out["before"] = before

    # "Moved" means the CHILD (or an operator intervening in the repo)
    # landed something — never the supervisor's own bookkeeping. The
    # journal, escalation-log and rebaseline-act commits carry fixed
    # prefixes precisely so this measurement can exclude them.
    seg = git("log", "--format=%s", "%s..HEAD" % before)
    if seg.returncode != 0:
        refuse("cannot read the commit segment %s..HEAD: %s"
               % (before[:12], seg.stderr[-400:]))
    child_commits = [s for s in seg.stdout.splitlines()
                     if s.strip()
                     and not s.startswith("campaign: ")
                     and not s.startswith("gm(rebaseline): ")]
    out["moved"] = bool(child_commits)

    # Stagnation counts CHILD segments only, read from the journal the
    # steward keeps — supervisor commits never count as progress.
    trailing = 0
    for r in reversed(rows):
        if len(r) > 3 and r[3] == "no":
            trailing += 1
        else:
            break
    out["stagnant"] = 0 if out["moved"] else trailing + 1

    # Eligibility mirrors the worker's plan_read: done AND blocked both land
    # and satisfy dependants — a blocked lot's debt belongs to the lots that
    # follow it, so it must not freeze the rest of the programme.
    cur_text = open(os.path.join(ws, plan_rel), encoding="utf-8").read()
    plan = read_plan(cur_text)
    if plan is None:
        refuse("the contract no longer parses at HEAD — a child run left an "
               "unreadable plan, which is a stop, not something to skip over")
    lots = plan.get("lots") or []
    if not isinstance(lots, list) or not all(isinstance(l, dict) for l in lots):
        refuse("the contract's `lots` is not a list of lots — a stop, not "
               "something to skip over")
    landed = set(l.get("id") for l in lots if l.get("status") in ("done", "blocked"))
    eligible = False
    for lot in lots:
        if lot.get("status") in ("done", "blocked"):
            continue
        if all(d in landed for d in (lot.get("depends_on") or [])):
            eligible = True
            break
    out["exhausted"] = not eligible

    # Contract EXTENSIONS: any change to the plan in this child segment
    # beyond a lot flipping its own status. The worker is entitled to write
    # `status` and to add committed reports; adding or reshaping lots is an
    # act of programme AUTHORSHIP, and who approves that is a configured
    # policy, not a default.
    def normative(plan_obj):
        m = {}
        for l in (plan_obj or {}).get("lots") or []:
            m[l.get("id")] = {k: l.get(k) for k in
                              ("title", "intent", "exit_gate", "depends_on",
                               "rebaseline_allowed")}
        return m
    old_show = git("show", "%s:%s" % (before, plan_rel))
    old_plan = read_plan(old_show.stdout) if old_show.returncode == 0 else None
    old_lots, new_lots = normative(old_plan), normative(plan)
    exts = []
    for lid in new_lots:
        if lid not in old_lots:
            exts.append({"kind": "added", "lot": lid,
                         "title": (new_lots[lid].get("title") or "")})
        elif new_lots[lid] != old_lots[lid]:
            changed = [k for k in new_lots[lid] if new_lots[lid][k] != old_lots[lid][k]]
            exts.append({"kind": "modified", "lot": lid, "fields": changed})
    for lid in old_lots:
        if lid not in new_lots:
            exts.append({"kind": "removed", "lot": lid})
    out["extensions"] = exts

    # Pending ledger requests: machine-readable request blocks without a
    # matching act. A block that does not parse is NOT skipped — a request
    # the steward cannot read is an escalation, not a silence.
    ledger = os.path.join(ws, {{input.ledger_path}})
    requests, acts, failed = [], set(), set()
    if os.path.isfile(ledger):
        text = open(ledger, encoding="utf-8").read()
        for kind in ("request", "act"):
            marker = "<!-- iterion:rebaseline-%s" % kind
            pos = 0
            while True:
                start = text.find(marker, pos)
                if start < 0:
                    break
                end = text.find("-->", start)
                if end < 0:
                    break
                body = text[start + len(marker):end].strip()
                pos = end + 3
                try:
                    obj = json.loads(body)
                except ValueError as e:
                    if kind == "request":
                        requests.append({"id": "", "malformed": True,
                                         "error": str(e), "raw": body[:400]})
                    continue
                if kind == "request":
                    requests.append(obj)
                else:
                    acts.add(obj.get("id"))
    # Requests the steward already refused or failed on stay refused: acting
    # again would loop on the same wall. The record of refusal is committed.
    esc_log = os.path.join(campaign_dir, "escalations.jsonl")
    if os.path.isfile(esc_log):
        with open(esc_log, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for line in f:
                try:
                    item = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                rid = item.get("request_id")
                if rid:
                    failed.add(rid)
    out["pending"] = [r for r in requests
                      if r.get("malformed") or
                      (r.get("id") and r["id"] not in acts and r["id"] not in failed)]

    print(json.dumps(out))

  language: py
  input: observe_input
  output: observe_state

## steward — the OTHER authority, mechanised but still separate.
##
## It executes a re-record if and only if: the request is well-formed, the
## requesting lot's contract allows a re-baseline, the tree is clean, and —
## the criterion that carried an entire supervised campaign — the observed
## diff equals the announced set EXACTLY. Then the full counter-test replays
## on the committed tree, and a red there reverts the act. Everything else
## becomes an escalation item, written and committed before anyone is asked
## anything. An act the campaign's wall would not let end before the handoff
## must start is deferred, never started: the request stays pending.
tool steward:
  script: |
    import datetime
    import json
    import os
    import subprocess
    import time

    # bool and json input fields render as JSON literals (true/false/null),
    # so the three JSON atoms need their Python names before interpolation.
    true, false, null = True, False, None

    ws = {{vars.workspace_dir}}
    governance = {{vars.governance}}
    escalation = {{vars.escalation}}
    plan_rel = {{vars.plan_path}}
    ledger_rel = {{input.ledger_path}}
    verify_rel = {{input.verify_path}}
    before = {{input.before}}
    moved = {{input.moved}}
    extensions = list({{input.extensions}} or [])
    pending = list({{input.pending}} or [])

    campaign_rel = os.path.join(".modernize", "campaign")
    campaign_dir = os.path.join(ws, campaign_rel)
    journal_rel = os.path.join(campaign_rel, "journal.tsv")
    esc_rel = os.path.join(campaign_rel, "escalations.jsonl")
    os.makedirs(campaign_dir, exist_ok=True)

    out = {"acts": 0, "escalations": [], "pause_required": False, "notice": ""}
    items = []
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")

    # The campaign's wall, as the engine holds the campaign to it: from 90% of
    # it the engine refuses to start a tool node, finalize's included, and a
    # campaign this node carries there ends with no handoff. An act — the
    # record, then the counter-test, each up to the lot's wall — starts only
    # where it ends, at those walls, RESERVE_S before that point: the act's
    # own git work after them (1800 s at their walls), this node's journal
    # commit (360 s), then loop_gate, lot_room and finalize starting. One that
    # does not fit is deferred: named in the escalation log, left pending in
    # the ledger for the next campaign, never refused; a campaign with a
    # longer wall (--max-duration) acts on it. A run with no wall (0) defers
    # nothing. What this node does outside its acts, lot_room_s keeps after
    # every lot.
    RESERVE_S = 2400
    run_wall = float({{run.max_duration_seconds}} or 0)
    handoff_by = None
    if run_wall > 0:
        handoff_by = time.monotonic() + run_wall * 0.9 - float({{run.elapsed_seconds}} or 0) - RESERVE_S

    def git(*args, timeout=120):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws] + list(args),
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)

    def sh(cmd, timeout=3600):
        """(exit code, tail, seconds). A command past its wall answers None —
        a red, never a crash of this node — and its whole process group is
        killed: killing the shell alone leaves what it started writing to
        the tree. The output goes to a file, not to memory, and is read back
        with replacement characters where it is not UTF-8."""
        import signal
        import tempfile
        import time
        started = time.monotonic()
        with tempfile.TemporaryFile() as log_f:
            p = subprocess.Popen(cmd, shell=True, cwd=ws, stdout=log_f,
                                 stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, start_new_session=True)
            try:
                code = p.wait(timeout=timeout)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                try:
                    os.killpg(p.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
                except ProcessLookupError:
                    pass
                p.wait()
                code = None
            seconds = time.monotonic() - started
            log_f.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            log_f.seek(max(0, log_f.tell() - 4000))
            tail = log_f.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
        if code is None:
            tail = "timed out after %ds; last output:\n%s" % (timeout, tail)
        return code, tail, seconds

    # History and the tree are read as committed and as on disk — by this
    # node's reads and by the commands it runs alike: a replace ref and the
    # repository's stat-cache keys are the worker's to write, and they make
    # git answer with something else. The keys are appended after the
    # entries the engine passes in the same channel. "" when done.
    def neutralise_git():
        os.environ["GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS"] = "1"
        slot = os.environ.get("GIT_CONFIG_COUNT", "0") or "0"
        if not slot.isdigit():
            return ("GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=%r is not a count, so git's stat cache could not be "
                    "turned off: the tree cannot be read as it is on disk" % slot)
        slot = int(slot)
        for key, value in (("core.checkStat", "default"), ("core.trustctime", "true"),
                           ("core.ignoreStat", "false"), ("core.fsmonitor", "false"),
                           ("core.untrackedCache", "false")):
            os.environ["GIT_CONFIG_KEY_%d" % slot] = key
            os.environ["GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_%d" % slot] = value
            slot += 1
        os.environ["GIT_CONFIG_COUNT"] = str(slot)
        return ""

    # The engine's own noise — the .claude/ mirror, a tool node's script
    # scratch, the canonical list it passes in $ITERION_TREE_NOISE — is never
    # a pass's work (#1364).
    def is_noise(path):
        if path == ".claude" or path.startswith(".claude/") \
                or os.path.basename(path).startswith(".iterion-script-"):
            return True
        import shlex
        for spec in shlex.split(os.environ.get("ITERION_TREE_NOISE", "")):
            for pre in (":(exclude,top)", "(exclude,top)"):
                if spec.startswith(pre):
                    spec = spec[len(pre):]
                    break
            if spec.endswith("*") and path.startswith(spec[:-1]):
                return True
            if path == spec or path.startswith(spec.rstrip("/") + "/"):
                return True
        return False

    # What of the working tree no landing carries, measured the way the
    # judging bot measures it: every untracked file git does not ignore
    # (whatever status.showUntrackedFiles says), every submodule's own work,
    # and every index entry flagged assume-unchanged or skip-worktree —
    # which `git status` trusts over the disk. A sparse checkout's absent
    # skip-worktree entries are where it keeps them.
    def unlanded():
        st = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "-c", "core.quotePath=false", "status",
                             "--porcelain", "-z", "--untracked-files=all", "--ignore-submodules=none"],
                            capture_output=True, timeout=300)
        if st.returncode != 0:
            return ["git status failed: %s" % st.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[-200:]]
        paths, entries = set(), st.stdout.split(b"\0")
        i = 0
        while i < len(entries):
            entry, i = entries[i], i + 1
            if len(entry) < 4:
                continue
            path = os.fsdecode(entry[3:])
            if entry[:1] in (b"R", b"C"):
                i += 1  # -z: a rename's or a copy's source follows as an entry of its own
            if not is_noise(path):
                paths.add(path)
        lv = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "ls-files", "-v", "-z"], capture_output=True, timeout=120)
        for entry in lv.stdout.split(b"\0"):
            if len(entry) < 3:
                continue
            tag, path = entry[:1].decode(), os.fsdecode(entry[2:])
            if not (tag in ("S", "s") or tag.islower()) or is_noise(path):
                continue
            paths.add("%s (flagged in the index)" % path)
        return sorted(paths)

    # The tree before a record, and the record's observed diff: what no
    # landing carries, the engine's noise left out.
    def changed_paths():
        return unlanded()

    def restore_clean(observed):
        # Only the record's own writes exist: the tree was verified clean
        # before it ran. Tracked files go back to HEAD; files the record
        # CREATED are removed one by one — never a recursive clean.
        # iterion's own tree under .claude/ (skills, plugin files, the settings
        # hooks it injects) is never the record's write, and never reverted here.
        # The tracked ones are read in one call, whatever their number: the
        # steward's reserve holds this node's git work at its walls, which a
        # call per path would leave unbounded.
        git("checkout", "--", ".", ":(exclude).claude")
        if not observed:
            return
        listed = git("ls-files", "-z", "--", *[":(literal)" + p for p in observed])
        if listed.returncode != 0:
            raise RuntimeError("git ls-files could not say which recorded paths are tracked, so "
                               "the record's own files cannot be told apart: %s"
                               % listed.stderr.strip()[-400:])
        tracked = set(listed.stdout.split("\0"))
        for p in observed:
            full = os.path.join(ws, p)
            if p not in tracked and os.path.isfile(full):
                os.remove(full)

    head_after_child = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()

    # ── Contract extensions, under the configured governance ──
    for ext in extensions:
        if governance == "bot":
            items.append({"ts": now, "type": "contract_extension",
                          "disposition": "accepted_in_flight",
                          "detail": ext,
                          "note": "listed at the head of the handoff for review"})
        else:
            items.append({"ts": now, "type": "contract_extension",
                          "disposition": "pending_approval", "detail": ext})

    # ── Pending ledger requests ──
    git_view = neutralise_git()
    tree_dirty = bool(git_view) or bool(changed_paths())
    if pending and tree_dirty:
        items.append({"ts": now, "type": "dirty_tree",
                      "detail": git_view or changed_paths()[:20],
                      "note": "a child run left uncommitted changes; the "
                              "steward acts only on committed trees, so every "
                              "pending request is deferred"})
    elif pending:
        # The plan tells the steward which lots may request a re-baseline,
        # and the wall each lot's commands run under.
        plan_lots, plan_top = {}, {}
        try:
            import shutil
            # PATH ONLY: a fallback under <ws>/.devbox would run a binary the
            # target repository supplied (#1799). Absent, the OSError raised
            # here is what the except below records as plan_unreadable.
            yq = shutil.which("yq")
            if not yq:
                raise OSError("yq is not on PATH; the workspace is never a source for it")
            raw = subprocess.run([yq, "-o=json", os.path.join(ws, plan_rel)],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
            if raw.returncode == 0:
                plan_top = json.loads(raw.stdout)
                if not isinstance(plan_top, dict):
                    raise ValueError("the plan is a %s at its top, not a mapping of keys"
                                     % type(plan_top).__name__)
                lots = plan_top.get("lots")
                if lots is not None and (not isinstance(lots, list)
                                         or not all(isinstance(l, dict) for l in lots)):
                    raise ValueError("the plan's `lots` is not a list of lots")
                for l in (lots or []):
                    plan_lots[l.get("id")] = l
        except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
            items.append({"ts": now, "type": "plan_unreadable", "detail": str(e)})
            plan_lots = None

        # The wall lot_verify gives the lot's commands: `gate_timeout_s` on the
        # lot, else at the top level, else 3600. A value it would refuse is
        # named, and nothing runs under a wall this node guessed.
        def wall_of(l):
            """(seconds, defect): seconds is None when the value is unreadable."""
            for src, holder in (("lot %s" % l.get("id"), l), ("the top level", plan_top)):
                if isinstance(holder, dict) and "gate_timeout_s" in holder:
                    raw = holder.get("gate_timeout_s")
                    if isinstance(raw, bool) or not isinstance(raw, int) or not 1 <= raw <= 86400:
                        return None, "unreadable gate_timeout_s at %s (%r)" % (src, raw)
                    return raw, ""
            return 3600, ""

        for req in pending if plan_lots is not None else []:
            rid = req.get("id") or "?"
            if req.get("malformed") or not req.get("id"):
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "malformed_request",
                              "request_id": rid, "detail": req})
                continue
            expected = sorted(set(req.get("expected_paths") or []))
            if not expected:
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "malformed_request",
                              "request_id": rid,
                              "detail": "a request that announces no expected "
                                        "paths cannot be verified"})
                continue
            lot = plan_lots.get(req.get("lot"))
            if lot is None:
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "refused_unknown_lot",
                              "request_id": rid, "detail": req.get("lot")})
                continue
            if not lot.get("rebaseline_allowed"):
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "refused_by_contract",
                              "request_id": rid, "lot": req.get("lot"),
                              "note": "the lot declares rebaseline_allowed: "
                                      "false — that flag is the assertion "
                                      "being tested, and a request against it "
                                      "means the lot overflowed"})
                continue

            wall, wall_defect = wall_of(lot)
            if wall is None:
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "refused_unreadable_wall",
                              "request_id": rid, "lot": req.get("lot"),
                              "detail": wall_defect})
                continue
            if handoff_by is not None and time.monotonic() + 2 * wall > handoff_by:
                # `request`, not `request_id`: observe reads a logged
                # request_id as a refusal and never acts on it again.
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "deferred_by_wall",
                              "request": rid, "lot": req.get("lot"),
                              "note": "the record and its counter-test take up to %ds (twice "
                                      "the lot's wall of %ds), and %ds remain before the "
                                      "campaign's handoff must start (90%% of its wall of %ds, "
                                      "less %ds for the act's own git work, this node's journal "
                                      "commit and the handoff to start), from "
                                      "which the engine refuses to start a tool node — the request "
                                      "stays pending for the next campaign, and a longer wall "
                                      "(--max-duration) gives its act room"
                                      % (2 * wall, wall, max(0, int(handoff_by - time.monotonic())),
                                         int(run_wall), RESERVE_S)})
                continue
            code, log, _ = sh("%s --record" % verify_rel, wall)
            observed = changed_paths()
            if code != 0:
                restore_clean(observed)
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "record_failed",
                              "request_id": rid, "detail": log[-1500:]})
                continue
            if observed != expected:
                restore_clean(observed)
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "diff_mismatch",
                              "request_id": rid,
                              "expected": expected, "observed": observed,
                              "note": "the observed diff is not the announced "
                                      "one — nothing was committed"})
                continue

            # Observed == announced: the act is committed, then judged.
            sha_before_act = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
            act = {"id": rid, "lot": req.get("lot"),
                   "recorded_paths": observed, "ts": now}
            with open(os.path.join(ws, ledger_rel), "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write("\n<!-- iterion:rebaseline-act\n%s\n-->\n"
                        % json.dumps(act, ensure_ascii=False))
            git("add", "--", ledger_rel, *observed)
            git("commit", "-m",
                "gm(rebaseline): act %s — %d reference(s) re-recorded, "
                "observed diff matches the announcement" % (rid, len(observed)))

            gate_code, gate_log, _ = sh(verify_rel, wall)
            restore_clean(changed_paths())
            if gate_code == 0:
                verdict = {"id": rid, "gate": "green",
                           "sha": git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip(), "ts": now}
                with open(os.path.join(ws, ledger_rel), "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write("\n<!-- iterion:rebaseline-verdict\n%s\n-->\n"
                            % json.dumps(verdict, ensure_ascii=False))
                git("add", "--", ledger_rel)
                git("commit", "-m",
                    "gm(rebaseline): full counter-test green after act %s" % rid, "--", ledger_rel)
                out["acts"] += 1
            else:
                # The act did not hold: the committed tree it produced fails
                # its own net. The act is unwound entirely — these commits
                # are the steward's own, on a tree verified clean before it
                # touched anything — and the failure is escalated.
                git("reset", "--hard", sha_before_act)
                items.append({"ts": now, "type": "gate_red_after_record",
                              "request_id": rid, "detail": gate_log[-1500:]})

    # ── Journal row + escalation log, committed as supervisor bookkeeping ──
    if items:
        with open(os.path.join(ws, esc_rel), "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for item in items:
                f.write(json.dumps(item, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")

    journal = os.path.join(ws, journal_rel)
    if not os.path.isfile(journal):
        with open(journal, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("run\tts\tbefore\tmoved\tacts\tafter\n")
    with open(journal, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        run_n = sum(1 for l in f if l.strip()) # header counts as row 0's slot
    head_now = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    with open(journal, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write("%d\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%d\t%s\n"
                % (run_n, now, before[:12], "yes" if moved else "no",
                   out["acts"], head_now[:12]))
    # The journal commit takes its own two files and nothing else: what a
    # child run left in the index is not the supervisor's to land.
    mine = [journal_rel] + ([esc_rel] if items else [])
    git("add", "--", *mine)
    git("commit", "-m", "campaign: run %d — child moved=%s, steward acts=%d, "
        "escalations=%d" % (run_n, "yes" if moved else "no", out["acts"], len(items)), "--", *mine)

    # ── Pause policy — configuration applied to facts, in readable code ──
    needs_approval = any(i.get("disposition") == "pending_approval" for i in items)
    # A deferral asks the operator nothing: the request waits for the next
    # campaign, and the handoff lists it.
    other_items = [i for i in items if i.get("type") not in ("contract_extension", "deferred_by_wall")]
    out["pause_required"] = bool(
        needs_approval or (escalation == "interactive" and other_items))
    out["escalations"] = items
    out["notice"] = ("%d act(s), %d escalation item(s)" % (out["acts"], len(items)))
    print(json.dumps(out))

  language: py
  input: steward_input
  output: steward_state

## record_answer — the operator's answer goes where every other fact went:
## in the committed record, before the campaign moves again.
tool record_answer:
  script: |
    import datetime
    import json
    import os
    import subprocess

    ws = {{vars.workspace_dir}}
    action = {{input.action}}
    note = {{input.note}}
    esc_rel = os.path.join(".modernize", "campaign", "escalations.jsonl")
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")

    with open(os.path.join(ws, esc_rel), "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps({"ts": now, "type": "operator_answer",
                            "action": action, "note": note},
                           ensure_ascii=False) + "\n")
    subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "add", "--", esc_rel],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "commit", "-m",
                    "campaign: operator answered escalation — %s" % action],
                   capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    print(json.dumps({"stop": action == "stop"}))

  language: py
  input: escalate_answer
  output: answer_state

## finalize — the campaign's verdict, computed the way a third party would.
##
## Final counter-test on the committed tree; requalification of blocked
## lots (each DISTINCT gate command played once on the final tree, the
## verdict projected per lot — a lot blocked mid-programme often blocks on
## a cause the finished tree has closed); handoff written and committed,
## why the campaign ended under its title, extensions first. The handoff
## is written before the campaign's wall: a command the wall would outlive is
## cut, and named as cut, not as a verdict.
tool finalize:
  script: |
    import datetime
    import json
    import os
    import subprocess
    import time

    ws = {{vars.workspace_dir}}
    plan_rel = {{vars.plan_path}}
    verify_rel = {{input.verify_path}}
    ended_by = {{input.ended_by}}
    campaign_rel = os.path.join(".modernize", "campaign")
    campaign_dir = os.path.join(ws, campaign_rel)
    handoff_rel = os.path.join(campaign_rel, "handoff.md")
    requal_rel = os.path.join(campaign_rel, "requalification.json")
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")

    out = {"handoff_path": handoff_rel, "oracle_green": False,
           "lots_done": 0, "lots_blocked": 0, "requalified": 0, "notice": ""}

    def git(*args, timeout=120):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws] + list(args),
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)

    def sh(cmd, timeout=3600):
        """(exit code, tail, seconds). A command past its wall answers None —
        a red, never a crash of this node — and its whole process group is
        killed: killing the shell alone leaves what it started writing to
        the tree. The output goes to a file, not to memory, and is read back
        with replacement characters where it is not UTF-8."""
        import signal
        import tempfile
        import time
        started = time.monotonic()
        with tempfile.TemporaryFile() as log_f:
            p = subprocess.Popen(cmd, shell=True, cwd=ws, stdout=log_f,
                                 stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, start_new_session=True)
            try:
                code = p.wait(timeout=timeout)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                try:
                    os.killpg(p.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
                except ProcessLookupError:
                    pass
                p.wait()
                code = None
            seconds = time.monotonic() - started
            log_f.seek(0, os.SEEK_END)
            log_f.seek(max(0, log_f.tell() - 4000))
            tail = log_f.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
        if code is None:
            tail = "timed out after %ds; last output:\n%s" % (timeout, tail)
        return code, tail, seconds

    # The campaign's wall, as the engine holds this node to it: started under
    # the wall, the node is killed at the wall; started past it, inside the
    # exit grace, the run has no time of its own left. Every command below
    # runs under its own wall or under what the campaign's wall leaves before
    # the handoff must be written, whichever is shorter, so the handoff is
    # written whatever the commands take — and a command the campaign's wall
    # stopped is reported as that, never as a verdict on the tree. WRITE_S is
    # the handoff's own time after the last command, whichever the wall cut:
    # the tree measured again once — after the gates, or after suites that
    # started (git status and ls-files, 420 s at their walls) — HEAD read
    # after the suites (120 s), the handoff added and committed (two git
    # calls under 120 s walls): 780 s at most. A run with no wall (0) stops
    # nothing.
    WRITE_S = 900
    run_wall = float({{run.max_duration_seconds}} or 0)
    write_by = None
    if run_wall > 0:
        write_by = time.monotonic() + max(0.0, run_wall - float({{run.elapsed_seconds}} or 0)) - WRITE_S

    def run_cmd(cmd, wall):
        """One command's run, under its own wall or what the campaign's wall
        leaves, whichever is shorter: {rc, seconds, tail, wall, cut, started}.
        `cut` is set when the campaign's wall stopped it — rc None then reads
        as that, not as the command outliving its own wall — and `started`
        is False when no time was left to start it at all."""
        room = wall if write_by is None else min(wall, int(write_by - time.monotonic()))
        if room <= 0:
            return {"rc": None, "seconds": 0.0, "wall": wall, "cut": True, "started": False,
                    "tail": "not started: the campaign's wall left no time for it"}
        code, tail, seconds = sh(cmd, room)
        return {"rc": code, "seconds": seconds, "wall": wall, "tail": tail,
                "cut": code is None and room < wall, "started": True}

    def cut_note(run):
        """What the campaign's wall did to a run it stopped."""
        if not run.get("started", True):
            return "the campaign's wall left no time to start it"
        return ("the campaign's wall cut it after %.0fs, under its own wall of %ds"
                % (run["seconds"], run["wall"]))

    # History and the tree are read as committed and as on disk — by this
    # node's reads and by the commands it runs alike: a replace ref and the
    # repository's stat-cache keys are the worker's to write, and they make
    # git answer with something else. The keys are appended after the
    # entries the engine passes in the same channel. "" when done.
    def neutralise_git():
        os.environ["GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS"] = "1"
        slot = os.environ.get("GIT_CONFIG_COUNT", "0") or "0"
        if not slot.isdigit():
            return ("GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=%r is not a count, so git's stat cache could not be "
                    "turned off: the tree cannot be read as it is on disk" % slot)
        slot = int(slot)
        for key, value in (("core.checkStat", "default"), ("core.trustctime", "true"),
                           ("core.ignoreStat", "false"), ("core.fsmonitor", "false"),
                           ("core.untrackedCache", "false")):
            os.environ["GIT_CONFIG_KEY_%d" % slot] = key
            os.environ["GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_%d" % slot] = value
            slot += 1
        os.environ["GIT_CONFIG_COUNT"] = str(slot)
        return ""

    # The engine's own noise — the .claude/ mirror, a tool node's script
    # scratch, the canonical list it passes in $ITERION_TREE_NOISE — is never
    # a pass's work (#1364).
    def is_noise(path):
        if path == ".claude" or path.startswith(".claude/") \
                or os.path.basename(path).startswith(".iterion-script-"):
            return True
        import shlex
        for spec in shlex.split(os.environ.get("ITERION_TREE_NOISE", "")):
            for pre in (":(exclude,top)", "(exclude,top)"):
                if spec.startswith(pre):
                    spec = spec[len(pre):]
                    break
            if spec.endswith("*") and path.startswith(spec[:-1]):
                return True
            if path == spec or path.startswith(spec.rstrip("/") + "/"):
                return True
        return False

    # What of the working tree no landing carries, measured the way the
    # judging bot measures it: every untracked file git does not ignore
    # (whatever status.showUntrackedFiles says), every submodule's own work,
    # and every index entry flagged assume-unchanged or skip-worktree —
    # which `git status` trusts over the disk. A sparse checkout's absent
    # skip-worktree entries are where it keeps them.
    def unlanded():
        st = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "-c", "core.quotePath=false", "status",
                             "--porcelain", "-z", "--untracked-files=all", "--ignore-submodules=none"],
                            capture_output=True, timeout=300)
        if st.returncode != 0:
            return ["git status failed: %s" % st.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[-200:]]
        paths, entries = set(), st.stdout.split(b"\0")
        i = 0
        while i < len(entries):
            entry, i = entries[i], i + 1
            if len(entry) < 4:
                continue
            path = os.fsdecode(entry[3:])
            if entry[:1] in (b"R", b"C"):
                i += 1  # -z: a rename's or a copy's source follows as an entry of its own
            if not is_noise(path):
                paths.add(path)
        lv = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "ls-files", "-v", "-z"], capture_output=True, timeout=120)
        for entry in lv.stdout.split(b"\0"):
            if len(entry) < 3:
                continue
            tag, path = entry[:1].decode(), os.fsdecode(entry[2:])
            if (tag in ("S", "s") or tag.islower()) and not is_noise(path):
                paths.add("%s (flagged in the index)" % path)
        return sorted(paths)

    # PATH ONLY (preflight's find_yq carries the reason): a fallback under
    # <ws>/.devbox would run a binary the target repository supplied (#1799).
    def find_yq():
        import shutil
        return shutil.which("yq") or ""

    head = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    git_view = neutralise_git()
    dirty = [git_view] if git_view else unlanded()

    # The plan is read as committed: the final tree is HEAD, and what the
    # working tree holds instead is reported as not judged. An unread plan is
    # said — in the handoff and in the notice — rather than its counts
    # reading as zeros and its suites as "none declared".
    plan, top_keys, unread = {}, [], ""
    yq = find_yq()
    shown = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", ws, "show", "HEAD:%s" % plan_rel],
                           capture_output=True, timeout=60)
    if not yq:
        unread = "yq is not on PATH"
    elif shown.returncode != 0:
        unread = ("it is not committed at HEAD: %s"
                  % shown.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()[-300:])
    else:
        import tempfile
        with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("wb", suffix=".yaml", delete=False) as tf:
            tf.write(shown.stdout)
        try:
            raw = subprocess.run([yq, "-o=json", tf.name], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        finally:
            os.unlink(tf.name)
        if raw.returncode != 0:
            unread = "it does not parse: %s" % raw.stderr.strip()[-300:]
        else:
            try:
                plan = json.loads(raw.stdout)
                top = json.loads(raw.stdout, object_pairs_hook=lambda pairs: pairs)
            except ValueError as e:
                # yq prints one JSON document per YAML document: a stray `---`
                # makes two, which no reader takes as one contract.
                plan, unread = {}, "it is not one YAML document (%s)" % str(e)[:200]
            else:
                if not isinstance(plan, dict):
                    plan, unread = {}, ("it is a %s at its top, not a mapping of keys"
                                        % type(plan).__name__)
                elif plan.get("lots") is not None and not (
                        isinstance(plan.get("lots"), list)
                        and all(isinstance(l, dict) for l in plan.get("lots"))):
                    plan, unread = {}, "its `lots` is not a list of lots"
                else:
                    ids = [l.get("id") for l in (plan.get("lots") or [])]
                    bad = [i for i in ids if not isinstance(i, str) or not i]
                    dup = sorted({i for i in ids if ids.count(i) > 1})
                    if bad:
                        # An id is a KEY: the requalification verdicts, the
                        # steward's plan and the child runs' only_lot all read
                        # through it.
                        plan, unread = {}, ("a lot carries no non-empty string id (%s)"
                                            % json.dumps(bad[:3], default=str))
                    elif dup:
                        plan, unread = {}, ("duplicate lot id(s) %s — an id is a key"
                                            % ", ".join(dup))
                    else:
                        top_keys = [k for k, _ in top]
    plan_read = not unread
    lots = plan.get("lots") or []
    done = [l for l in lots if l.get("status") == "done"]
    blocked = [l for l in lots if l.get("status") == "blocked"]
    todo = [l for l in lots if l.get("status") not in ("done", "blocked")]
    out["lots_done"], out["lots_blocked"] = len(done), len(blocked)

    # The wall lot_verify gives each of a lot's commands: `gate_timeout_s` on
    # the lot, else at the top level, else 3600. A value it would refuse is
    # named here, and nothing runs under a wall this node guessed.
    def wall_of(l):
        """(seconds, defect): seconds is None when the value is unreadable."""
        for src, holder in (("lot %s" % l.get("id"), l), ("the top level", plan)):
            if isinstance(holder, dict) and "gate_timeout_s" in holder:
                raw = holder.get("gate_timeout_s")
                if isinstance(raw, bool) or not isinstance(raw, int) or not 1 <= raw <= 86400:
                    return None, "unreadable gate_timeout_s at %s (%r)" % (src, raw)
                return raw, ""
        return 3600, ""

    # Every lot's gate ran the oracle and the suites under its own wall: on the
    # final tree they get the longest of them, so what no gate timed out is not
    # timed out here — and each blocked lot reads every run against its own.
    top_wall, top_wall_defect = wall_of({})
    final_wall = max([w for w, _ in [wall_of(l) for l in lots] if w is not None]
                     + ([top_wall] if top_wall is not None else []), default=None)

    # Requalification: distinct commands once each, verdicts projected.
    # Same contract surface as modernize's plan_read: a scalar exit_gate is
    # one command, a sequence several; any other shape is unreadable and the
    # lot carrying it is NOT requalifiable — iterating it raw would shell
    # out one command per character (or per mapping key) and manufacture a
    # verdict nobody's gate ever rendered.
    def gate_commands(l):
        """(commands, defect): commands is None when the gate is not
        requalifiable, and defect names WHICH refusal, verbatim for the
        committed verdict — the two causes are distinct diagnoses."""
        gates = l.get("exit_gate") or []
        if isinstance(gates, str):
            gates = [gates]
        if not isinstance(gates, list) or not all(isinstance(g, str) for g in gates):
            shape = (type(l.get("exit_gate")).__name__ if not isinstance(gates, list)
                     else "list holding %s" % type(next(
                         g for g in gates if not isinstance(g, str))).__name__)
            return None, "unreadable exit_gate (%s)" % shape
        # A command containing an INTERIOR newline is refused by modernize's
        # reader (its transport is newline-joined); accepting it here would
        # let this supervisor requalify a lot the executing bot cannot even
        # read. Strip first — yq hands every block/folded scalar with a
        # trailing newline, and a single-command block is legitimate.
        gates = [g.strip() for g in gates]
        gates = [g for g in gates if g]
        if any("\n" in g for g in gates):
            return None, "multi-line exit_gate command"
        if any("\0" in g for g in gates):
            return None, "exit_gate command holding a NUL byte"
        return gates, ""

    # The repository's suites, declared once at the top of the contract and run
    # at every lot's gate (modernize's lot_verify), read the same way: one
    # command as a string or a list of single-line ones. Run once on the final
    # tree; a blocked lot requalifies only when they are green there too.
    def suite_commands(doc, keys=()):
        """(commands, defect): commands is None when the declaration cannot be
        read, [] when the contract declares none. `keys` are the contract's
        top-level keys in order: suites declared twice is unreadable."""
        if list(keys).count("suites") > 1:
            return None, "suites declared more than once at the top of the contract"
        if not isinstance(doc, dict) or "suites" not in doc:
            return [], ""
        raw = doc.get("suites")
        cmds = [raw] if isinstance(raw, str) else raw
        if not isinstance(cmds, list) or not all(isinstance(c, str) for c in cmds):
            return None, "unreadable suites (%s)" % type(raw).__name__
        cmds = [c.strip() for c in cmds if c.strip()]
        if not cmds:
            return None, "suites declared with no command"
        if any("\n" in c for c in cmds):
            return None, "multi-line suites command"
        if any("\0" in c for c in cmds):
            return None, "suites command holding a NUL byte"
        return cmds, ""

    suites, suites_defect = suite_commands(plan, top_keys)
    if suites and final_wall is None:
        suites, suites_defect = None, top_wall_defect

    # The commands run in lot_verify's order — a blocked lot's gate, then the
    # suites, then the oracle — on the committed tree, measured before the
    # gates and again before the suites: what a command leaves behind is not
    # what the next one judges.
    requal = {"head": head[:12], "commands": {}, "suites": [], "lots": {}}
    commands = {}
    if not dirty and blocked:
        # A command two lots share runs once, under the longer of their
        # walls; each lot then reads the run against its own wall.
        for l in blocked:
            wall, gates = wall_of(l)[0], gate_commands(l)[0]
            if wall is None or not gates:
                continue
            for g in gates:
                commands[g] = max(commands.get(g, 0), wall)
        for c, wall in commands.items():
            r = run_cmd(c, wall)
            requal["commands"][c] = {"rc": r["rc"], "seconds": r["seconds"], "wall": wall,
                                     "tail": r["tail"][-400:], "cut": r["cut"],
                                     "started": r["started"]}
    left_by_gates = unlanded() if commands else []

    suites_red, suites_cut = None, None
    # Set when the suites ran green: what they left, and whether HEAD still
    # names the tree the handoff judges.
    left_by_suites, head_moved = [], False
    if not plan_read:
        suites_summary = "NOT READ: the plan could not be read (%s)." % unread
    elif suites is None:
        suites_summary = "NOT RUN: %s." % suites_defect
    elif not suites:
        suites_summary = "None declared."
    elif dirty:
        suites_summary = "NOT JUDGED: the tree was not committed."
    elif left_by_gates:
        suites_summary = ("NOT JUDGED: the gate commands left a tree no landing carries (%s)."
                          % "; ".join(left_by_gates[:10]))
    else:
        for c in suites:
            r = run_cmd(c, final_wall)
            requal["suites"].append({"command": c, "rc": r["rc"], "seconds": r["seconds"],
                                     "wall": r["wall"], "tail": r["tail"][-400:], "cut": r["cut"],
                                     "started": r["started"]})
            if r["cut"]:
                suites_cut = requal["suites"][-1]
                break
            if r["rc"] != 0:
                suites_red = requal["suites"][-1]
                break
        # Suites the campaign's wall left no time to start left the tree as it
        # was last measured — after the gates, or before any command when no
        # gate ran: measuring it again would spend the time the handoff keeps
        # for itself.
        if any(r["started"] for r in requal["suites"]):
            left_by_suites = unlanded()
            head_moved = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip() != head
        suites_summary = ("GREEN — every command exited 0" if suites_red is None
                          else "RED — `%s` %s" % (suites_red["command"],
                                                  "timed out" if suites_red["rc"] is None
                                                  else "exited %d" % suites_red["rc"]))
        if suites_cut is not None:
            suites_summary = ("NOT JUDGED: `%s`: %s."
                              % (suites_cut["command"], cut_note(suites_cut)))
        elif suites_red is None and head_moved:
            suites_summary = "NOT JUDGED: the suites moved HEAD."
        elif suites_red is None and left_by_suites:
            suites_summary = ("NOT JUDGED: the suites left a tree no landing carries (%s)."
                              % "; ".join(left_by_suites[:10]))

    # The gate judges COMMITTED trees only. A dirty tree at the end is
    # reported as exactly that — a verdict over a tree that exists nowhere
    # would be worth nothing.
    verdict_line, oracle_state, oracle_run = "", "red", None
    if dirty:
        oracle_state = "unjudged"
        oracle_summary = ("NOT JUDGED: %d uncommitted change(s) at campaign "
                          "end — the counter-test only judges committed "
                          "trees. Files: %s" % (len(dirty), "; ".join(dirty[:10])))
    elif left_by_gates:
        oracle_state = "unjudged"
        oracle_summary = ("NOT JUDGED: the gate commands left a tree no landing carries (%s)."
                          % "; ".join(left_by_gates[:10]))
    elif left_by_suites:
        oracle_state = "unjudged"
        oracle_summary = ("NOT JUDGED: the suites left a tree no landing carries (%s)."
                          % "; ".join(left_by_suites[:10]))
    elif head_moved:
        oracle_state = "unjudged"
        oracle_summary = "NOT JUDGED: the suites moved HEAD."
    elif final_wall is None:
        oracle_state = "not run"
        oracle_summary = "NOT RUN: %s." % top_wall_defect
    else:
        oracle_run = run_cmd(verify_rel, final_wall)
    # An oracle the campaign's wall stopped delivered no verdict on the tree.
    if oracle_run is not None and not oracle_run["started"]:
        oracle_state = "not run"
        oracle_summary = "NOT RUN: the campaign's wall left no time to start the oracle."
    elif oracle_run is not None and oracle_run["cut"]:
        oracle_state = "unjudged"
        oracle_summary = ("NOT JUDGED: the campaign's wall cut the oracle after %.0fs, under "
                          "its own wall of %ds." % (oracle_run["seconds"], oracle_run["wall"]))
    elif oracle_run is not None:
        code, log = oracle_run["rc"], oracle_run["tail"]
        out["oracle_green"] = code == 0
        oracle_state = "green" if code == 0 else "red"
        for line in reversed((log or "").splitlines()):
            line = line.strip()
            if line.startswith("{") and line.endswith("}"):
                verdict_line = line
                break
        oracle_summary = ("GREEN (exit 0)" if code == 0
                          else "RED (%s) — tail:\n\n```\n%s\n```"
                          % ("timed out" if code is None else "exit %d" % code, log[-1500:]))

    if not dirty and blocked:
        for l in blocked:
            lid = l.get("id")
            gates, defect = gate_commands(l)
            wall, wall_defect = wall_of(l)
            if gates is not None and wall is None:
                gates, defect = None, wall_defect
            if gates is None:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "%s — not requalifiable" % defect}
                continue
            if not gates:
                # Zero commands ran; "every gate command green" over an empty
                # set is the vacuous claim this report exists to refuse.
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "no exit_gate declared — not requalifiable"}
                continue

            def past(run):
                """Why a run is red for THIS lot: its exit, or a run longer
                than the lot's own wall — lot_verify's GATE_TIMEOUT. A run the
                campaign's wall stopped is no verdict, red or green."""
                if run.get("cut"):
                    return ""
                if run["rc"] is None:
                    return "timed out"
                if run["rc"] != 0:
                    return "exit %d" % run["rc"]
                if run["seconds"] > wall:
                    return "ran %.1fs, past this lot's wall of %ds" % (run["seconds"], wall)
                return ""
            reds = [(g, past(requal["commands"][g])) for g in gates if past(requal["commands"][g])]
            cuts = [g for g in gates if requal["commands"][g].get("cut")]
            if reds:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "still blocked (%s)" % reds[0][1], "red": reds[0][0]}
            elif cuts:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "NOT JUDGED — `%s`: %s — not requalifiable on "
                                                  "this run" % (cuts[0], cut_note(requal["commands"][cuts[0]]))}
            elif suites is None:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "%s — not requalifiable" % suites_defect}
            elif left_by_gates or left_by_suites:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "the commands left a tree no landing "
                                                  "carries — not requalifiable"}
            elif head_moved:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "the suites moved HEAD — not requalifiable"}
            elif suites_cut is not None:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "NOT JUDGED — its gate is green, the repository's "
                                                  "suites were not judged (`%s`: %s) — not "
                                                  "requalifiable on this run"
                                                  % (suites_cut["command"], cut_note(suites_cut))}
            elif suites and (suites_red is not None
                             or any(past(r) for r in requal["suites"])):
                red = suites_red or next(r for r in requal["suites"] if past(r))
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "still blocked — its gate is green, the "
                                                  "repository's suites are not (%s)" % past(red),
                                       "red": red["command"]}
            else:
                requal["lots"][lid] = {"verdict": "REQUALIFIED — every gate command%s green "
                                                  "on the final tree"
                                                  % (" and every suite" if suites else "")}
        out["requalified"] = sum(1 for v in requal["lots"].values()
                                 if v["verdict"].startswith("REQUALIFIED"))
        with open(os.path.join(ws, requal_rel), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            json.dump(requal, f, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)

    # The handoff. Extensions FIRST: they are decisions a bot took in
    # flight, and this document exists so a human re-takes them.
    esc_items = []
    esc_path = os.path.join(campaign_dir, "escalations.jsonl")
    if os.path.isfile(esc_path):
        with open(esc_path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for line in f:
                try:
                    esc_items.append(json.loads(line))
                except ValueError:
                    esc_items.append({"type": "unparseable_log_line",
                                      "raw": line[:200]})
    exts = [i for i in esc_items if i.get("type") == "contract_extension"]
    others = [i for i in esc_items if i.get("type") != "contract_extension"]

    journal_rows = []
    jpath = os.path.join(campaign_dir, "journal.tsv")
    if os.path.isfile(jpath):
        with open(jpath, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            journal_rows = [l.rstrip("\n") for l in f if l.strip()]

    h = []
    h.append("# Campaign handoff — %s" % now)
    h.append("")
    h.append("Why the campaign ended: %s" % ended_by)
    h.append("")
    if plan_read:
        h.append("Final tree: `%s`. Programme: %d done, %d blocked, %d not "
                 "reached, of %d lots." % (head[:12], len(done), len(blocked),
                                           len(todo), len(lots)))
    else:
        h.append("Final tree: `%s`. Programme: NOT READ — the plan at `%s` could "
                 "not be read (%s); no count below is a zero." % (head[:12], plan_rel, unread))
    h.append("")
    h.append("## 1. Contract extensions (%d) — review these first" % len(exts))
    h.append("")
    if exts:
        h.append("The worker changed the PROGRAMME itself during the "
                 "campaign. Under `governance: bot` each one was accepted in "
                 "flight and is listed here for a human to re-take; under "
                 "`governance: human` each one paused the campaign and its "
                 "approval is recorded below.")
        h.append("")
        for i in exts:
            h.append("- `%s` — %s (%s)" % (json.dumps(i.get("detail"),
                     ensure_ascii=False), i.get("disposition"), i.get("ts")))
    else:
        h.append("None: not one lot was added, reshaped or removed beyond "
                 "its own status field.")
    h.append("")
    h.append("## 2. Escalations and answers (%d)" % len(others))
    h.append("")
    for i in others:
        h.append("- `%s`" % json.dumps(i, ensure_ascii=False)[:500])
    if not others:
        h.append("None.")
    h.append("")
    h.append("## 3. Run journal")
    h.append("")
    h.append("```tsv")
    h.extend(journal_rows)
    h.append("```")
    h.append("")
    h.append("## 4. Final behavioural verdict")
    h.append("")
    h.append(oracle_summary)
    h.append("")
    h.append("The repository's suites on the final tree: %s" % suites_summary)
    if verdict_line:
        h.append("")
        h.append("```json")
        h.append(verdict_line)
        h.append("```")
    h.append("")
    h.append("## 5. Requalification of blocked lots")
    h.append("")
    if requal["lots"]:
        for lid, v in requal["lots"].items():
            h.append("- **%s** — %s%s" % (lid, v["verdict"],
                     (" (red: `%s`)" % v.get("red")) if v.get("red") else ""))
        h.append("")
        h.append("Detail (every distinct command, rc and tail): `%s`." % requal_rel)
    elif dirty:
        h.append("Not run: the tree was not committed.")
    else:
        h.append("No blocked lot to requalify.")
    h.append("")

    os.makedirs(campaign_dir, exist_ok=True)
    with open(os.path.join(ws, handoff_rel), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write("\n".join(h))
    # The handoff commit takes its own files and nothing else: what a child
    # run left in the index is not the supervisor's to land.
    mine = [handoff_rel] + ([requal_rel] if requal["lots"] else [])
    git("add", "--", *mine)
    suites_state = ("none" if suites_summary == "None declared."
                    else suites_summary.split(":")[0].split(" — ")[0].lower().rstrip("."))
    git("commit", "-m", "campaign: handoff — %s, oracle %s, suites %s"
        % ("%d done, %d blocked" % (len(done), len(blocked)) if plan_read else "plan NOT READ",
           oracle_state, suites_state), "--", *mine)

    # The closing review shows this notice: why the campaign ended leads it,
    # so a campaign that ran no lot says so before any count reads as zero.
    if plan_read:
        out["notice"] = ("ended: %s — %d done, %d blocked (%d requalify on the final tree), "
                         "oracle %s, suites %s" % (ended_by, len(done), len(blocked),
                                                   out["requalified"], oracle_state, suites_state))
    else:
        out["notice"] = ("ended: %s — the plan at %s was not read (%s): lots_done, lots_blocked "
                         "and requalified are unset, not zero; oracle %s"
                         % (ended_by, plan_rel, unread, oracle_state))
    print(json.dumps(out))

  language: py
  input: finalize_input
  output: finalize_state

## loop_gate — continue while something can still land. Pure conjunction of
## measured facts; the fall-through also serves loop and budget exhaustion.
compute loop_gate:
  output: loop_state
  expr:
    proceed: "!outputs.observe.exhausted && outputs.observe.stagnant < vars.stagnation_stop"
    reason: "if(outputs.observe.exhausted, 'programme exhausted: no eligible lot remains', if(outputs.observe.stagnant >= vars.stagnation_stop, 'stagnation: consecutive child runs landed nothing', 'continue'))"

## lot_room — start a lot only where one can finish. The child budgets
## itself from its own .bot (modernize's 12h wall), runs in THIS run's
## sandbox, and nothing interrupts it once started. A lot started late
## therefore outlives what the campaign can still do: from 90% of the wall
## the engine refuses to start a tool node, so observe is refused and the
## campaign ends with no handoff; on Kubernetes the shared sandbox pod is
## killed at the wall plus 30 min, mid-lot. Every lot the campaign's edges
## start passes here, the first one too; a wall with no room left for one
## hands off instead, and `reason` — which the handoff opens on — says so
## with the figures and the two levers: a wall that never had room for one
## lot starts none, which no operator should have to infer from an empty
## journal. A resume of a campaign whose lot failed re-runs run_lot, not
## this node: the child judges the same predicate where it starts
## (modernize's campaign_room, over the clock run_lot passes), so a lot that
## no longer fits is not started there either. `reason` restates `room`'s
## predicate (a field cannot read its sibling); the bots package holds the
## two, and the child's, to agree. An unbounded wall (0) always has room.
##
## The wall read here is the run's live one, a live raise included. A
## Kubernetes sandbox pod takes its deadline once, at its creation, from the
## wall the run was launched with: a live raise past that wall does not move
## it, and a lot this node admits on the raised wall dies with the pod. On
## Kubernetes, a longer campaign is launched with its wall (--max-duration).
compute lot_room:
  output: room_state
  expr:
    room: "run.max_duration_seconds <= 0 || run.elapsed_seconds + vars.lot_room_s < run.max_duration_seconds * 0.9"
    reason: "if(run.max_duration_seconds <= 0 || run.elapsed_seconds + vars.lot_room_s < run.max_duration_seconds * 0.9, 'room for a lot', if(loop.campaign_loop.iteration == 0, 'NO LOT STARTED', 'no lot after the ' + loop.campaign_loop.iteration + ' this campaign ran') + ': the elapsed time (' + floor(run.elapsed_seconds) + ' s) plus lot_room_s (' + vars.lot_room_s + ' s, the time one lot may take) reaches 90% of the campaign wall (' + floor(run.max_duration_seconds * 0.9) + ' s of ' + floor(run.max_duration_seconds) + ' s), from which the engine refuses to start a tool node. Lots known to be shorter start under `--var lot_room_s=<seconds>`; a longer wall under `--max-duration`.')"

## run_lot — the worker, one lot per child run.
##
## No `output:` is read from the child ON PURPOSE: the supervisor believes
## nothing a run says about itself. Progress is measured in git by the next
## node, exactly as it would be measured by a third party. No `isolated:`
## either — the child works on THIS checkout; that is what it is for, and
## the workspace-safety guard is right to serialise it.
##
## The campaign's clock travels with each start: the mapping renders when
## run_lot runs, a resumed run_lot included, and the child's campaign_room
## starts the lot only on lot_room's own predicate over these figures.
subbot run_lot:
  source: "../modernize/main.bot"
  with {
    workspace_dir: "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    plan_path: "{{vars.plan_path}}",
    max_passes: "{{vars.lot_max_passes}}",
    campaign_elapsed_s: "{{run.elapsed_seconds}}",
    campaign_wall_s: "{{run.max_duration_seconds}}",
    campaign_lot_room_s: "{{vars.lot_room_s}}",
  }

## ─── Workflow ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
workflow campaign:

  entry: preflight

  ## No `sandbox:` block (ADR-082): the platform sandboxes by default, and
  ## the target's toolchain comes from ITS OWN devbox/devcontainer. This
  ## bundle declares only yq and python3 — the supervisor stays repo- and
  ## stack-agnostic; every judged command is declared by the target repo.

  ## The child budgets itself (a subbot's budget does not compose with the
  ## parent's): this budget bounds the SUPERVISOR — its records, gates and
  ## bookkeeping — plus the campaign's wall clock, the one axis that counts
  ## the children's time. The wall the run is launched with also sets the
  ## deadline of the sandbox pod the lots share on Kubernetes (that wall plus
  ## 30 min, fixed when the pod is created: a live raise does not move it).
  ## The engine checks the wall before each campaign node, never inside a
  ## running lot, so lot_room — and the child's campaign_room where a resume
  ## starts a lot — starts one only while it still fits before 90% of it.
  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "48h"
    max_cost_usd: 25

  preflight -> lot_room

  lot_room -> run_lot when room

  lot_room -> finalize when not room with {
    verify_path: "{{outputs.preflight.verify_path}}",
    ended_by: "{{outputs.lot_room.reason}}",
  }

  run_lot -> observe with {
    base: "{{outputs.preflight.campaign_base}}",
    ledger_path: "{{outputs.preflight.ledger_path}}",
  }

  observe -> steward with {
    before: "{{outputs.observe.before}}",
    moved: "{{outputs.observe.moved}}",
    extensions: "{{outputs.observe.extensions}}",
    pending: "{{outputs.observe.pending}}",
    ledger_path: "{{outputs.preflight.ledger_path}}",
    verify_path: "{{outputs.preflight.verify_path}}",
  }

  ## Exhaustive pair on one bool: pause, or straight to the loop decision.
  steward -> escalate when pause_required with {
    items: "{{outputs.steward.escalations}}",
    context: "{{outputs.steward.notice}}",
  }

  steward -> loop_gate when not pause_required

  escalate -> record_answer with {
    action: "{{outputs.escalate.action}}",
    note: "{{outputs.escalate.note}}",
  }

  record_answer -> finalize when stop with {
    verify_path: "{{outputs.preflight.verify_path}}",
    ended_by: "an operator answered stop at an escalation",
  }

  record_answer -> loop_gate when not stop

  ## Continue while something can still land; the unguarded fall-through
  ## also serves loop exhaustion and the budget guard's declined back-edge —
  ## the campaign always leaves through its own handoff.
  loop_gate -> finalize when not proceed with {
    verify_path: "{{outputs.preflight.verify_path}}",
    ended_by: "{{outputs.loop_gate.reason}}",
  }

  loop_gate -> lot_room as campaign_loop("{{vars.max_lots}}")

  loop_gate -> finalize with {
    verify_path: "{{outputs.preflight.verify_path}}",
    ended_by: "the campaign loop took no lot after crossing {{loop.campaign_loop.iteration}} of {{loop.campaign_loop.max}} (max_lots): at max_lots the loop is spent; below it, its budget guard declined the next lot on the campaign's budget, and the run's events name the axis",
  }

  finalize -> handoff_review with {
    handoff_path: "{{outputs.finalize.handoff_path}}",
    summary: "{{outputs.finalize.notice}}",
  }

  handoff_review -> done
